PROPOSAL -- NOT APPLIED, NOT BUILT, NOT RUN.
reTS (game DLL) change that lets the Mech be truly un-disarmable and unable to take a weapon.

Why it is a proposal. With tsm_loadoutrework 1 the plugin already refuses the drop key and the
weapon bar's drop for the Mech, clears the use key while a weapon is in reach, refuses a touch, and
undoes a disarm (it sees the weapon gone at the next look, hands it back with the spare rounds it had
and removes the thrown one). That is a repair after the fact: the DLL still performs the disarm, so for
the length of a look (a quarter second) the Mech is bare-handed and a player standing on the thrown gun
could in principle grab it before it is removed. The DLL is the one place that can refuse both outright.

How the DLL learns who is a Mech. A plugin-owned flag in an entvars field the DLL does not use for a
player: pev->fuser4 (grep of dlls, cl_dll, game_shared and pm_shared finds no player use of fuser4; the
clientdata copies in client.cpp are cd->vuser*, a different structure). The plugin sets it to 1.0 for a
Mech each time it hands the kit over (lr_arm_begin) and to 0.0 for everyone else. TSInit does not touch
it, so the plugin owns its life.

Plugin half (to add to ts_matchmode.sma when this is built; harmless before, a DLL that ignores the
field changes nothing), in lr_arm_begin():

	set_pev(id, pev_fuser4, lr_is_mech(id) && get_pcvar_num(g_cv_lr) ? 1.0 : 0.0);

DLL half:

--- a/dlls/ts_gun.cpp
+++ b/dlls/ts_gun.cpp
@@ bool CBasePlayer::DropWeapon(int type, float flDamage)
 	CBasePlayer *pPlayer = this;
 	CTSGun *pGun = GetPlayerTSGun(pPlayer);
 	if (!pGun)
 		return false;
 
+	// tsm_loadoutrework: the plugin marks a Mech (pev->fuser4) -- it is neither disarmed (-2) nor lets go of
+	// a weapon on its own (the drop key, drop_<n>).
+	if (pev->fuser4 != 0.0f && CVAR_GET_FLOAT("tsm_loadoutrework") != 0.0f)
+		return false;
+
 	// disarm (-2) can't take the flag or bare hands
 	if ((pGun->m_weaponType == TSW_FLAG || pGun->m_weaponType < 1) && type == -2)
 		return false;

A death scatter does not come through CBasePlayer::DropWeapon with a negative type, so a Mech that dies still
leaves his weapon.

--- a/dlls/ts_player.cpp
+++ b/dlls/ts_player.cpp
@@ bool CBasePlayer::PickUpWeapon(int type, TSItem *pStatus, int iAttach, bool bSelect, bool bRecordOwner)
 	CTSGun *pGun = GetPlayerTSGun(this); // orig: this->m_pActiveItem gated on m_iId == 0x10
 	if (!pGun || type < 0 || type >= TSW_COUNT)
 		return false;
+	// tsm_loadoutrework: a Mech takes nothing from the floor (the plugin hands it its weapon itself, with
+	// ts_giveweapon, which does not come through here as a pickup of a map or dropped weapon).
+	if (pev->fuser4 != 0.0f && CVAR_GET_FLOAT("tsm_loadoutrework") != 0.0f && pStatus != NULL)
+		return false;
 	if (TS_IsCustomWeapon(type) && !H9_CustomWeaponsOn())
 		return false; // h9_customweapons 0: TS 3.0's own roster, which has no weapon 39 or 40

Notes for whoever builds it.
 - pStatus != NULL is true for a dropped gun (CTSWorldGun::GetWeapon passes its m_status) and false for a
   buy, a map weapon pickup (CTSGroundWeapon::GetWeapon passes NULL) and the M61 give. A map weapon
   (ts_groundweapon) is therefore NOT covered by that clause; the plugin's use-key and touch guard
   covers it until the clause is widened (drop "&& pStatus != NULL" if no Mech should ever pick up a map
   weapon either -- ts_giveweapon from the plugin reaches TakeWeapon, not PickUpWeapon, so it is safe).
 - Verify with a Mech on a map with a ts_groundweapon within reach, a disarm by a melee hit past the
   threshold (katana 30, melee class 25, others slotCost clamped 20..50), and the drop key.
 - CVAR_GET_FLOAT in a hot path: cache it per frame if profiling cares; the cvar is read only when the
   flag is set, so only for a Mech.
